{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a429143b3ed7379b5","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01BKEg3WjMYzasdUnF8hLuaZ","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-EI5ScE/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-3eT73m/8d463897-d2e4-446a-b9bb-aaf6d62cb9b1/tasks/a429143b3ed7379b5.output","summary":"I read both files completely and, since prebuilt `.so`s were already present, I verified my source-level reasoning against the actual disassembly of all three binaries (C `-O0`, Rust debug, Rust release). No files were modified and no builds were run.\n\n## Summary verdict\n\n**The Rust source is a faithful value-level translation.** I found no input `(param1..param4)` or `flags` value, and no `initial_capacity`/`capacity`/`size` value, for which the Rust returns a different *integer result* than the C. All the integer-semantics traps you listed are handled correctly (verified below). The divergences I did find are all in the *side-effect / crash-mode / codegen* domain, and two of them are real.\n\nFiles: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-3eT73m/c_src/src/lib.c`, `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-3eT73m/translation/src/lib.rs`, `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-3eT73m/translation/Cargo.toml`.\n\n---\n\n## D1 — `add_element`: `arr->size` increment is ordered BEFORE the element store in C, AFTER it in Rust. (Confidence: certain that the code differs; low-medium that a harness detects it)\n\nC is `arr->data[arr->size++] = value;`. GCC at `-O0` (the C lib is built with `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=` empty, i.e. no `-O`) emits, in `c_src/build/libharvest-work-3eT73m.so` at `0x12d7`:\n\n```\n12d7: lea 0x1(%rax),%rcx      ; new size\n12df: mov %rcx,0x8(%rdx)      ; arr->size = old+1   <-- FIRST\n12ee: mov %eax,(%rdx)         ; data[old] = value   <-- SECOND\n```\n\nRust `lib.rs:150-152` forces the opposite order (`let idx = size; *data.add(idx) = value; size = idx+1;`) and both Rust builds do the store first.\n\n**Trigger:** any call where the element store faults but the caller survives. Exact recipe:\n```c\nint *p = mmap(NULL, 4096, PROT_READ, MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);\nDynamicArray a = { p, 0, 4 };          // size < capacity, so no expand\n/* install SIGSEGV handler + siglongjmp */\nadd_element(&a, 7);\n```\nC: after recovery `a.size == 1`. Rust: `a.size == 0`. Also visible in a core dump, or with `data` pointing into a `mprotect(PROT_READ)` page.\n\n---\n\n## D2 — Rust DEBUG build aborts where C returns normally: misaligned-pointer UB checks (Confidence: high; verified in the binary)\n\n`Cargo.toml` has no `[profile.dev]` override, so the debug build has `debug-assertions = true`, and rustc 1.94 emits `Assert(PointerAlignment)` + `Assert(NullPointerDereference)` for **every raw-pointer deref**. I confirmed the panic targets by relocation:\n\n* `0x4bde7` = `core::panicking::panic_misaligned_pointer_dereference`\n* `0x4bdce` = `core::panicking::panic_null_pointer_dereference`\n* `0x4bd93` = `core::panicking::panic_cannot_unwind` (the `extern \"C\"` abort shim, called at the top of every exported fn)\n\nThese are `panic_nounwind` → **immediate `abort()`**, not a recoverable panic.\n\nConcrete check sites in `translation/target/debug/libmatrixsum_lib.so`:\n\n| function | site | check |\n|---|---|---|\n| `add_element` | `0x124d1`, `0x1252a`, `0x12594`, `0x125fd`, `0x126ec` | `arr & 7 != 0` → abort |\n| `add_element` | `0x1267a` | `(arr->data + size*4) & 3 != 0` → abort |\n| `add_element` | `0x12684` | `arr->data + size*4 == NULL` → abort |\n| `expand_array` | `0x128fe`, `0x12981`, `0x12a2d`, `0x12a80` | `arr & 7 != 0` → abort |\n| `free_array` | `0x12b2c` | `arr & 7 != 0` → abort |\n| `matrixsum` | `0x130d6`, `0x1314a` sites | same checks (unreachable — `arr` comes from `malloc`) |\n\n**Trigger A (cleanest — misaligned `data`, aligned `arr`):**\n```c\nunsigned char buf[64];\nDynamicArray a;                 /* stack-aligned to 8 */\na.data = (int*)(buf + 1);       /* 4-byte-misaligned */\na.size = 0; a.capacity = 8;\nint r = add_element(&a, 0x11223344);\n```\nC: unaligned 4-byte store succeeds on x86-64, returns `1`, `a.size == 1`. Rust debug: `panic_misaligned_pointer_dereference{ required: 4, found: buf+1 }` → SIGABRT. Rust release: matches C.\n\n**Trigger B (misaligned `DynamicArray*`):**\n```c\nunsigned char raw[64];\nDynamicArray *p = (DynamicArray*)(raw + 1);\np->data = malloc(16); p->size = 0; p->capacity = 4;\nadd_element(p, 7);      /* or expand_array(p), or free_array(p) */\n```\nC: works, returns `1`. Rust debug: `panic_misaligned_pointer_dereference{ required: 8, ... }` → SIGABRT.\n\n**Trigger C (`data == NULL`, `size < capacity`) — this one is a plausible \"corrupted struct\" test:**\n```c\nDynamicArray a = { NULL, 0, 4 };\nadd_element(&a, 7);\n```\nC: `arr->data[0] = 7` → **SIGSEGV** (signal 11). Rust debug: `panic_null_pointer_dereference` message on stderr + `panic in a function that cannot unwind` → **SIGABRT** (signal 6). Different termination signal and different stderr. Rust release matches C (SIGSEGV).\n\nNote the *good* news I verified here: `ptr::add`'s `assert_unsafe_precondition!` is `#[cfg(debug_assertions)]` on **core's own** build, so it is *absent* from the distributed toolchain. The debug build emits a plain `shl $0x2 / add` at `0x1265a` with no overflow trap, so a caller-crafted `size >= 2^61` wraps the address exactly like C rather than panicking. That was my top suspicion and it is **not** a divergence.\n\n---\n\n## D3 — Rust RELEASE build elides the `DynamicArray` heap allocation inside `matrixsum` (Confidence: certain the codegen differs; medium that it's observable)\n\nLLVM 21 inlined `init_array`/`add_element`/`free_array` into `matrixsum` and then removed the struct allocation entirely (SROA'd into registers). Compare the allocator traffic for `matrixsum(1,2,3,4)`:\n\n| | C | Rust release |\n|---|---|---|\n| calls | `malloc(24)`, `malloc(8)`, `realloc(p,16)`, `free(data)`, `free(arr)` | `malloc(8)`, `realloc(p,16)`, `free(data)` |\n\nRelease disassembly at `0x120a7`: `mov $0x8,%edi; call *…malloc` — the 24-byte struct `malloc` is gone; only one `free` at `0x12195`. (The exported standalone `init_array` at `0x12010` *does* still `malloc(0x18)`, so only the inlined copy is affected. The Rust **debug** `matrixsum` calls the real `init_array` and matches C exactly.)\n\n**Observable conditions:**\n1. **Allocator interposition / counting** (`LD_PRELOAD` a counting `malloc`, valgrind/massif, a `__malloc_hook`-style harness): C reports 2 allocations + 2 frees, Rust release reports 1 + 1.\n2. **Allocation-failure injection:** C returns `-1` if *either* `malloc(24)` or `malloc(8)` fails; Rust release returns `-1` only if the 8-byte one fails. So an injector that fails the Nth allocation, or specifically fails 24-byte requests, gets `-1` from C and a normal result (e.g. `1936`) from Rust. (With plain glibc this is unreachable — 24 and 8 bytes both land in the same 32-byte chunk class — which is what `ERRORS.md` E1/E10 assumes.)\n3. **Heap layout:** a subsequent `init_array(2)` returns a different address after `matrixsum` in Rust release vs C.\n\n---\n\n## D4 — Loss of symbol interposability for intra-library calls in RELEASE (Confidence: high mechanism, low likelihood of being tested)\n\nThe C `matrixsum` calls `init_array@plt`, `add_element@plt`, `process_flags@plt`, `calculate_matrix_checksum@plt`, `free_array@plt` — all through the PLT, so all five are **interposable**. Rust debug preserves this (`call *…(%rip)` against `R_X86_64_GLOB_DAT` relocations, e.g. `0x4f1c8 → expand_array@@Base`). Rust **release** inlines them, so nothing is interposable.\n\n**Trigger:** `LD_PRELOAD` (or a `RTLD_GLOBAL` library, or the test executable itself) defining e.g. `int process_flags(int){ return 99; }`. C `matrixsum(1,1,1,1)` → uses the interposed version; Rust release → uses the inlined original. This also means the C `matrixsum` is sensitive to a replaced `add_element`, and Rust release is not.\n\n---\n\n## Verified-clean findings (explicit per-function statements)\n\n* **`init_array` — no divergence.** `initial_capacity.wrapping_mul(4)` matches C's `size_t` product exactly. Release asm is `lea 0x0(,%rbx,4),%rdi`, i.e. mod-2^64 wrapping. `initial_capacity = 0` → both `malloc(0)` (same glibc, non-NULL). `initial_capacity = 2^62` → both `malloc(0)`, both return non-NULL with `capacity == 2^62`, `size == 0` (matches `ERRORS.md` E3). `SIZE_MAX` → `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFC` bytes → NULL in both. Field-write order on the failure path is identical (`data` is stored into the struct *before* the NULL test in both; `size`/`capacity` are left as malloc garbage and `arr` is freed in both). Struct malloc size 24 in both.\n* **`expand_array` — no divergence.** C's `(capacity*2)*4` and LLVM's folded `capacity*8` (`lea 0x0(,%r15,8),%rsi`) are equal mod 2^64 for all inputs, including `capacity = 2^63` (→ `realloc(p,0)`), `2^62` (→ `realloc(p,0)`), `SIZE_MAX` (→ `realloc(p, 2^64-8)` → NULL), and `capacity = 0` (→ `realloc(p,0)`, glibc frees `p` and returns NULL → both return `0` leaving `arr->data` identically dangling). On realloc failure both leave `data` and `capacity` untouched. `arr->data == NULL` → both do `realloc(NULL,n) == malloc(n)`.\n* **`free_array` — no value divergence** (only the D2 misaligned-`arr` debug abort). `arr == NULL` no-op, `data == NULL` → `free(NULL)` no-op, both.\n* **`process_flags` — no divergence at all.** `(x != 0) as c_int` is an exact match for `!!x`; `&` on negative operands is identical two's-complement. Verified for `-1`, `INT_MIN`, `INT_MAX`, `0xFFFFFFF0`: release asm is `and $1 / bt $1 / adc / shr $2 / bt $3 / adc`, which is exactly popcount of bits 0-3. Max result 4, so no overflow is even possible.\n* **`calculate_matrix_checksum` — no divergence.** **Not** constant-folded: both release (`0x11f50`) and debug (`0x1276c`) load `matrix`'s address from the GOT and read all 48 bytes, so `dlsym(\"matrix\")` writes are honoured identically. The `i += 1`/`j += 1` overflow traps (`seto`/`jo` at `0x127a9`, `0x1289a`) and the `panic_bounds_check` sites (`0x127f8`, `0x1282c`) are provably unreachable (`i∈0..3`, `j∈0..4`). The empty-parameter-list C declaration is ABI-identical to `extern \"C\" fn() -> c_int` on x86-64 SysV (no `%al` convention, extra args ignored). `wrapping_add` correctly models GCC's `-O0` wrapping of the signed sum, so a caller who pokes `matrix` to force signed overflow gets the same answer.\n* **`matrixsum` — no value divergence.** Final expression order and associativity match (`imul/imul/add/and $0xfff/add` in C at `0x155a`; `shl $4 / (x<<8)-x / add / and $0xfff / add` in Rust at `0x1211f`). Default result for all-nonzero params: `sum*16 + 4*255 + 916`. `matrixsum(INT_MIN,INT_MIN,INT_MIN,INT_MIN)` = `0 + 1020 + 916 = 1936` in both. The realloc-failure retry path is faithful: `add_element(p3)` fails without bumping `size`, then `add_element(p4)` re-attempts `realloc(old_data, 16)` — release asm reproduces this exactly at `0x120e9`, including reusing the *old* `data` pointer and summing only `size` elements.\n* **`matrix` global — ABI-compatible.** `nm -D` diff between the C `.so` and the Rust release `.so` is **empty** (I re-ran it): same 8 symbols, `matrix` is `OBJECT GLOBAL`, 48 bytes, in writable `.data` in both. Only cosmetic difference: the C `.data` section has `sh_addralign 32` (GCC's array alignment) vs Rust's `8`; not observable through the ABI.\n* **`DynamicArray` layout — matches.** `#[repr(C)]` `{*mut c_int, usize, usize}` = 24 bytes / align 8 / offsets 0,8,16, identical to the C struct; confirmed by the offsets used in both disassemblies (`(%rax)`, `0x8(%rax)`, `0x10(%rax)`).\n* **No unwinding can escape into C.** Every exported Rust fn has the `panic_cannot_unwind` shim, so debug-profile `panic = \"unwind\"` degrades to `abort`, same as release's `panic = \"abort\"`. This means D2 produces a clean abort rather than UB — but it is still an abort where C returns a value.\n\n### Suggested minimal fixes (if you want them)\n- D1: reorder `lib.rs:150-152` to write `(*arr).size` before the element store.\n- D2: read/write the struct through `core::ptr::read_unaligned`/`addr_of_mut!` + `write_unaligned` (or add `[profile.dev] debug-assertions = false`) so the debug profile stops enforcing alignment C never promised.\n- D3/D4: mark `init_array`, `expand_array`, `add_element`, `free_array`, `process_flags`, `calculate_matrix_checksum` `#[inline(never)]` (and/or call them through `core::hint::black_box`d fn pointers) to restore the PLT-visible call structure and prevent the allocation from being optimized away.","usage":{"total_tokens":91809,"tool_uses":26,"duration_ms":519878},"uuid":"cd43fe26-8d70-46a7-b65f-883ffc097fe3","session_id":"8d463897-d2e4-446a-b9bb-aaf6d62cb9b1"}
