ered out; finished in 0.02s\ntest result: ok. 3 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.49s\ntest result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.53s\ntest result: ok. 4 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.39s\n--- clean rebuild from scratch sanity ---\n    Finished `release` profile [optimized] target(s) in 0.01s","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_j3kqzfecfac4qi5d6hhieqb77r7hxh6bp5zvcxlfztkn2wedxa3a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 63 tests pass. Verification is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe library is small (one C translation unit, 8 exported symbols), so I verified it exhaustively rather than by sampling.\n\n**Phase A** — `SYMBOLS.md`, `ERRORS.md`, `CONFIGS.md` derived mechanically from the C. Symbol parity was already exact (8/8, `nm -D` diff empty, `matrix` 0x30 bytes in both) — nothing had been left untranslated.\n\n**Phases B–D** — 63 differential tests in 9 binaries, all loading *both* `.so`s via `libloading` and calling only through `dlsym`. Verified across 2 feature combos × 2 cdylib profiles × 5 C optimization levels.\n\n### Four real divergences found and fixed\n\nValue-level behaviour matched from the start. Everything I found was *outside* the return-value surface — which is exactly why ordinary tests miss it. I proved each one with a failing test before fixing it:\n\n1. **Side-effect order in `add_element`** — GCC compiles `data[size++] = value` by committing `size = old+1` *first*. The Rust stored the element first, so a faulting store left `size == 0` instead of `1`. Reordered to match.\n2. **Misaligned caller pointers** — the C makes no alignment promise (plain unaligned `mov`); the dev-profile Rust `abort()`ed via `Assert(PointerAlignment)`. Switched to `read_unaligned`/`write_unaligned`.\n3. **`data == NULL` with `size < capacity`** — C dies with `SIGSEGV`, Rust died with `SIGABRT`. Same fix plus `[profile.dev] debug-assertions = false`.\n4. **Release inliner elided an allocation** — LLVM inlined the helper chain into `matrixsum` and SROA'd the `DynamicArray` away, dropping `malloc(24)` entirely. That removes one of the two allocation-failure points that can return `-1`, and makes the helpers non-interposable. Fixed with `#[inline(never)]`; the release `.so` now carries `GLOB_DAT` relocations mirroring the C's PLT `JUMP_SLOT`s.\n\n### How I knew the tests were trustworthy\n\n`mutation_check.sh` injects 35 bugs into a *copy* of the crate, builds both profiles, and requires the suite to fail: **33 killed, 2 survivors** — and both survivors are provably semantics-preserving (`!!x` removed where `x ∈ {0,1}`, and where the value is only consumed as `if x != 0`), so the script asserts they *must* survive.\n\nThis is what exposed the divergences. My first suite passed everything, but mutation testing showed four blind spots: the harness reset `matrix` to its own constant before comparing (masking a wrong initializer); `free_array` returns `void` (masking a missing `free`); crashes kill the process (masking crash-mode differences); and balanced allocator traffic hides an elided allocation. Each is now covered by a dedicated test file.\n\nThree earlier \"divergences\" were **harness bugs, not translation bugs**, and are documented in `VERIFICATION.md`: concurrent tests clobbering the shared mutable `matrix` global, and `RLIMIT_DATA` (6 GiB) being a *per-process* budget shared by both `.so`s — holding two 4 GiB allocations live made the second fail for reasons unrelated to the code.\n\n`c_src/` is byte-for-byte unmodified (md5 verified); the only additions there are the `build/` directory the task instructed me to create."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":777,"cache_read_input_tokens":229449,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":777,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8d463897-d2e4-446a-b9bb-aaf6d62cb9b1","uuid":"7d3903bc-8df7-4a9d-924d-247b22f48f78","timestamp":"2026-08-28T04:01:59.575Z"}
